          "secNumber": "4.14",
          "heading": "Unit 4 Review and Methodological Synthesis",
          "content": "In Unit 4, we analyzed the computational and systems architectures underpinning distributed statistics for massive data. We examined the shared-nothing cluster model and formulated the MapReduce computing paradigm, showing how associative and commutative sufficient statistics (sample means, contingency tables, Gram matrices $\\mathbf{X}^T \\mathbf{X}$) can be aggregated in a single pass with $O(M)$ network communication. We detailed Apache Spark's in-memory computing architecture, Resilient Distributed Datasets (RDDs), lineage graphs, and memory persistence mechanisms that execute machine learning loops 100 times faster than disk-backed Hadoop. We analyzed the communication complexity vs. computational complexity trade-off, examining local SGD and federated averaging. We proved Zhang, Duchi, and Wainwright's statistical efficiency theorem for one-shot parameter averaging, showing that when $K \\le \\sqrt{N}$, distributed averaging achieves optimal centralized minimax efficiency. Finally, we studied columnar formats (Apache Parquet and Apache Arrow), ADMM distributed consensus optimization, parameter servers for massive embedding tables, local differential privacy (LDP), and vectorized query execution.",
