          "secNumber": "4.19",
          "heading": "Practical Rules for Distributed Statistical System Architecture",
          "content": "When engineering distributed big data pipelines for statistical modeling, practitioners must avoid common architectural pitfalls:\n1. **Avoid Distributed Overkill**: Never deploy a distributed Spark or Hadoop cluster for datasets smaller than 10 Gigabytes. Single-node multicore workstations executing vectorized C++/Python routines are significantly faster and incur zero cluster network serialization latency.\n2. **Prevent Shuffle Spills in Spark DataFrames**: Monitor Spark execution logs for shuffle memory spills. If partitions exceed worker RAM, increase the partition count (`spark.sql.shuffle.partitions`) to ensure that every partition fits comfortably in local cache.\n3. **Leverage Broadcast Joins for Dimension Lookups**: Ensure small metadata tables ($< 50 \\text{ MB}$) are broadcast to workers via `broadcast(df)` to eliminate expensive network shuffles of multi-terabyte fact tables.",
