          "secNumber": "5.12",
          "heading": "Unit 5 Review and Methodological Synthesis",
          "content": "In Unit 5, we developed first-order stochastic optimization theory for massive datasets. We derived the Robbins-Monro stochastic gradient descent update rule, proving that single-sample stochastic gradients $\\nabla f_i(\\mathbf{w})$ are strictly unbiased estimators of the full population gradient $\\nabla F(\\mathbf{w})$. We proved that under the Robbins-Monro step size schedule ($\\sum \\eta_t = \\infty, \\sum \\eta_t^2 < \\infty$), SGD converges almost surely at rate $O(1/\\sqrt{t})$ for general convex losses and $O(1/t)$ for strongly convex functions. We analyzed Mini-Batch SGD, showing that batch size $B$ scales hardware vectorization while reducing gradient variance by $1/B$. We studied accelerated optimizers: Polyak momentum, Nesterov accelerated gradient (proving the optimal $O(1/t^2)$ rate and Su-Boyd-Candès continuous ODE limit), AdaGrad, RMSprop, and Adam with bias correction. We developed variance-reduced stochastic optimization via SVRG and SAGA, proving linear convergence rates $O(\\rho^k)$ without step size decay. Finally, we analyzed Hogwild! lock-free asynchronous optimization, Amari's natural gradient descent with K-FAC, and communication-compressed SignSGD with error feedback.",
