          "secNumber": "7.20",
          "heading": "Stream Deduplication and Sliding-Window HyperLogLog",
          "content": "Standard HyperLogLog accumulates distinct cardinality over unbounded streams from epoch zero. In streaming operational environments, however, queries frequently demand sliding-window cardinality: \"How many unique visitors were active within the past 60 minutes?\" \n\nDirectly subtracting two HyperLogLog sketches is mathematically impossible because the maximum-zero operation $\\max(M[j], \\rho(w))$ is idempotent and non-invertible. To solve this challenge, Sliding HyperLogLog replaces each 6-bit register with a timestamped record $(v, t_{\\text{last}})$, storing the latest arrival timestamp $t_{\\text{last}}$ that yielded value $v$. When evaluating the sliding window $[T - W, T]$, the estimator discards any entries with $t_{\\text{last}} < T - W$ before computing the harmonic mean. This sliding-window architecture provides accurate streaming deduplication and unique visitor metrics across moving time horizons with constant bounded memory.",
